= Key Terms / Mappings
- *Subjunctive:* A grammatical form used to express wishes, commands, doubts, possibilities, or hypothetical situations rather than facts.
- *Frame*: A computational instance of a context.
- *Bongard Problem:* A problem where the objective is to spot the differences between the two sides consisting of six images each.
- *Focusing:* Description of some part ignoring everything else (nouns).
- *Filtering:* Description of viewing the contents (adjectives).
- *Mirabile dictum:* Strange to say.
- *Conceptual Mapping:* Succession of mappings of ideas onto each other.
- *Conceptual Skeleton:* Abstract structures connecting different ideas.
- *Forced Matching:* When the high level is so compelling that even if there is no lower-level continuation continuation of the map you still make one.

= Counter Case or Limitation
After reading the last chapter and the title of this one, I expected it would be about where he believes AI is going.
However, there was only a few pages of speculation at the end.
I think it would have been appropriate to speculate more especially on things like how it would affect the economy and day to day lives of people.

